In Discord.js, you can check when a message is sent by using the `messageCreate` event. This event is triggered whenever a message is created in a channel that your bot has access to. Here's a basic example of how you can set this up:

First, make sure you have Discord.js installed in your project. If you haven't installed it yet, you can do so by running:

```bash
npm install discord.js
```

Then, you can set up your bot to listen for the `messageCreate` event like this:

```javascript
const { Client, GatewayIntentBits } = require('discord.js');
const client = new Client({
    intents: [
        GatewayIntentBits.Guilds,
        GatewayIntentBits.GuildMessages,
        GatewayIntentBits.MessageContent, // This intent is required to read message content
    ]
});

client.on('messageCreate', (message) => {
    console.log(`Message received: ${message.content}`);
    // You can add more logic here to handle the message
});

client.login('your-bot-token-here');
```

In this example:
- We import the necessary classes from `discord.js`.
- We create a new client instance and specify the intents that the bot needs. The `GatewayIntentBits.MessageContent` intent is required to read the content of messages.
- We listen for the `messageCreate` event and log the content of the message to the console.
- Finally, we log in the bot with your bot token.

Make sure to replace `'your-bot-token-here'` with your actual bot token.

This setup will allow your bot to respond to messages as they are sent in channels where the bot has permission to read messages.
